In software development, testing is the process of verifying that an application works as expected and finding defects before releasing it to users.

Common Types of Testing
1. Unit Testing

Tests a single class, method, or function in isolation.

Example:

public int add(int a, int b){
    return a + b;
}

Test:

assertEquals(5, add(2,3));

Tools: JUnit, Mockito

2. Integration Testing

Tests whether multiple modules work correctly together.

Example:

Spring Boot Service ↔ Repository ↔ Database

Checks if data is saved and retrieved properly.

3. System Testing

Tests the complete application as a whole.

Example:

Login
Create User
Send SMS
View Reports

Everything is tested together.

4. Regression Testing

Ensures that existing functionality still works after new changes or bug fixes.

Example:
You fix a bug in the SMS module.

Now you test:

Login
Dashboard
User Management
SMS Sending

to ensure nothing else broke because of your change.

Interview Definition:

Regression testing is performed after code changes to verify that previously working functionality continues to work correctly.

5. Smoke Testing

A quick test to verify that the major features of a new build are working.

Example:

Application starts
Login works
Database connection works

If smoke testing fails, further testing is stopped.

6. Sanity Testing

Performed after a minor change or bug fix to verify that the specific functionality works.

Example:
A bug in OTP generation is fixed.

Test only OTP-related scenarios.

7. Functional Testing

Verifies that the application behaves according to business requirements.

Example:
Requirement:

User can send SMS to up to 1000 recipients.

Testing checks whether this requirement is met.

8. Non-Functional Testing

Tests quality attributes rather than functionality.

Includes:

Performance Testing
Load Testing
Security Testing
Usability Testing
9. Performance Testing

Checks application speed and responsiveness.

Example:
Dashboard should load within 2 seconds.

10. Load Testing

Checks behavior under expected user load.

Example:
1,000 users sending SMS simultaneously.

Tools:

JMeter
Gatling
11. Stress Testing

Checks behavior beyond normal limits.

Example:
10,000 users on a system designed for 1,000 users.

12. Security Testing

Finds vulnerabilities.

Example:

SQL Injection
XSS
CSRF
Authentication issues
13. User Acceptance Testing (UAT)

Performed by business users or clients before production release.

Example:
Ufone team verifies that the SMS portal satisfies all business requirements.

Smoke vs Sanity vs Regression
| Testing Type       | Purpose                        | Scope          |
| ------------------ | ------------------------------ | -------------- |
| Smoke Testing      | Check whether build is stable  | Broad, shallow |
| Sanity Testing     | Verify specific change/fix     | Narrow         |
| Regression Testing | Ensure old features still work | Broad, deep    |

Interview Example

Suppose you fixed a bug in the OTP module:

Sanity Testing → Test OTP functionality only.
Regression Testing → Test OTP + Login + Dashboard + SMS + Reports.
Smoke Testing → Verify the application starts and major features are accessible.

